===Etymology===
From the ending of the words {{m|en|quintuple}}, {{m|en|sextuple}}; from Latin {{m|la|-plus}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtʊpəl/|/ˈt͡ʃuːpəl/|/ˈtjuːpəl/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtuːpəl/|/ˈtʌpəl/}}
* {{rhymes|en|uːpəl|ʌpəl|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|set theory}} A [[finite]] [[sequence]] of [[term]]s.
#: {{hypo|en|n-tuple|q1=n terms|ordered pair|q2=two terms|triple|triplet|q3=three terms}}
# {{lb|en|databases}} A single [[row]] in a [[relational database]].
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[set]] of comma-separated [[value]]s passed to a program or operating system as a [[parameter]] to a [[function]] [[call]].
# {{lb|en|programming}} A fixed-size container [[data type]] similar to a [[list]] that can hold different types of elements.
#: {{ux|en|Both Python and Haskell have a '''tuple''' data type as well as a list data type.}}
#: {{ux|en|Unlike lists, '''tuples''' are not formed by [[cons]]ing.}}

